---
id: AP-016
type: rule
family: type-system-evasion
title: Zod z.unknown() in a schema (opt-in)
version: 1

severity: warning
confidence: medium
spectrum_position: 3

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx, .js, .jsx, .mjs, .cjs]
allowlist: ['*.d.ts', '**/__mocks__/**', '**/test/**/*.ts', '**/__tests__/**', '**/*.setup.ts', '**/*.setup.js', '**/explain/**']

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '\bz\.unknown\s*\('

related: [AP-003, AP-015]
enabled: true
opt_in: true
---

`z.unknown()` in a Zod schema accepts any value and validates nothing,
just like `z.any()` (AP-015) — but it is the least-bad escape hatch
because the resulting type is `unknown`, which forces consumers to
narrow before use rather than silently spreading like `any`. It is also
genuinely idiomatic as a leaf in a typed container — `z.record(z.string(),
z.unknown())` for a metadata bag, `value: z.unknown()` in a generic
cache schema — and this family's own guidance recommends parsing
external data into `unknown` and narrowing it.

For those reasons this rule is **opt-in** (`opt_in: true`): it is off by
default and only fires under `--include-opt-in` / a profile that
requests it. Enable it on a codebase that wants every schema field to
carry a real contract, where a `z.unknown()` signals an unfinished
schema rather than a deliberate one.

When it does fire: give the field the shape you actually depend on, or
keep `z.unknown()` and leave a `// TODO(types): ...` with a tracking
reference so the gap is visible rather than permanent. Like AP-015 the
detector is line-based, so a `z.unknown(` in a comment or string, or an
aliased Zod import, is not distinguished.
